Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "burmese-protection-mandate":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Burmese Protection Mandate",infoboxTitle:"Burmese Protection Mandate",portrait:{src:"assets/diagrams/burmese-federal-architecture.svg",alt:"Diagram of Burmese federal and external institutions",caption:"The mandate places a Thai supervisory apex above surviving Burmese governments while Japan retains the external guarantee."},
    lead:"The Burmese Protection Mandate is the common working name for the late-1990s order under which Burma remains a formally sovereign member of the Greater East Asian system while Thailand controls common security, heavy weapons, external alignment, strategic transport, and final arbitration. Its durability rests on preserving each Burmese nationality government beneath an authority that is unmistakably foreign and therefore not a victory for any one Burmese people.",
    canon:"The late-1990s mandate order, formal Burmese sovereignty, Thai control fields, survival of constituent institutions, High Commission, Council of Burmese States, Thai Security Command, and ultimate Japanese guarantee are established. Burmese Protection Mandate and the institutional titles are useful working names; exact legal titles, treaty date, officeholders, headquarters, budgets, and force ceilings remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origin",title:"Origin in exhaustion",html:"<p>After repeated federal settlements, partition, reunion, and renewed war, the <a href='final-burmese-crisis.html'>final Burmese crisis</a> exhausts Tokyo's willingness to invent another internal constitution. Japan authorizes a <a href='thai-intervention-burma.html'>Thai intervention</a> and delegates the daily burden of stabilization.</p>"},
      {id:"status",title:"Sovereignty without strategic freedom",html:"<p>Burma keeps its flag, citizenship, international personality, central and state governments, languages, schools, temples, local courts, and ordinary administration. It is not annexed, settled by Thai colonists, or placed beneath the Chakri king as a legal Burmese monarch.</p><p>Yet Bangkok exercises powers ordinarily associated with sovereignty wherever common security or external alignment is concerned.</p>"},
      {id:"commission",title:"Thai High Commission",html:"<p>The <a href='thai-high-commission-burma.html'>Thai High Commission in Burma</a> supervises security, diplomacy, budget settlement, transport corridors, inter-state disputes, and appointments to common security bodies. It normally leaves village, temple, school, and local-court government to Burmese institutions.</p>"},
      {id:"council",title:"Council of Burmese States",html:"<p>The <a href='council-burmese-states.html'>Council of Burmese States</a> brings Bamar, Shan, Kachin, Chin, Karen, Karenni, Mon, Arakanese, and other recognized governments into a common forum. Security, foreign, heavy-infrastructure, and boundary decisions require Thai confirmation.</p>"},
      {id:"security",title:"Thai Security Command",html:"<p>The <a href='thai-security-command-burma.html'>Thai Security Command</a> controls heavy weapons, military aviation, strategic communications, principal arsenals, trunk railway and river systems, ceasefire observation, and inter-state troop movements. Burmese forces retain infantry, police, border guards, and registered local-security arms.</p>"},
      {id:"japan",title:"Japanese external guarantee",html:"<p>Japan prevents outside powers from exploiting the intervention and prevents Thailand from annexing Burma or leaving the Japanese system. Tokyo has delegated labor, not surrendered primacy.</p>"},
      {id:"logic",title:"The external-apex logic",html:"<p>No Burmese nationality must concede that another nationality is the rightful national master. A Shan commander accepting a Thai ceasefire does not accept Bamar or Karen supremacy. The humiliation of foreign supervision becomes more tolerable than domestic subordination.</p>"},
      {id:"result",title:"Durability without reconciliation",html:"<p>The mandate freezes operational-scale conflict more successfully than its predecessors while preserving local grievances, anti-Thai nationalism, and disputed legitimacy. It is a functioning protectorate, not a reconciled Burmese nation.</p>"}
    ],related:[...burmeseMandateRelated,{href:"thai-high-commission-burma.html",kicker:"Executive body",label:"Thai High Commission"},{href:"burma-preservationist-break.html",kicker:"Imperial consequence",label:"The preservationist break"}],
    facts:[["Established","Late 1990s; exact date open"],["Protected state","Union of Burma"],["Protecting power","Thailand"],["Ultimate guarantor","Japan"],["Executive body","Thai High Commission in Burma; working title"],["Common forum","Council of Burmese States; working title"],["Security body","Thai Security Command; working title"],["Annexation","None"]]
  }),

  "final-burmese-crisis":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Final Burmese Crisis",eyebrow:"Burma · approximately 1994–1997",infoboxTitle:"Final Burmese Crisis",portrait:{src:"assets/diagrams/burmese-war-cycle.svg",alt:"Diagram of the recurring Burmese war and settlement cycle",caption:"The final crisis ends the recurring pattern by replacing another internal settlement with Thai supervision."},
    lead:"The Final Burmese Crisis was the last large breakdown of the recurring Burmese war-and-settlement cycle, conventionally dated to approximately 1994–1997. Unlike earlier crises, it did not produce another bespoke federal compromise. Japan authorized Thailand to secure the country's strategic infrastructure, restrict the rival armies, and impose the protectorate order later called the Burmese Protection Mandate.",
    canon:"The approximate 1994–1997 placement, exhaustion of previous settlements, Japanese decision against another internal compromise, Thai intervention, secured strategic nodes, restriction of major armies, and mandate outcome are established. Trigger, belligerent coalitions, battles, commanders, casualties, exact dates, and formal peace instrument remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"cycle",title:"The exhausted settlement cycle",html:"<p>The Companion War, Rangoon Settlement, Federal Crisis, Second Burmese War, Sovereign States Experiment, War of the Burmese States, and restored union each leave armed losers, mixed populations, disputed corridors, and a capital interpreted as ethnic domination.</p>"},
      {id:"breakdown",title:"Breakdown",html:"<p>Inter-state troop movements, heavy weapons, transport access, militia patronage, and disputes over common authority again push the Burmese order beyond ordinary arbitration. The exact initiating incident remains open.</p>"},
      {id:"japan",title:"Japanese exhaustion",html:"<p>Tokyo concludes that another constitution would merely redistribute grievances until the next mobilization. The decision marks a retreat from the belief that every national problem must be solved through internally authentic institutions.</p>"},
      {id:"thai",title:"Authorization of Thailand",html:"<p>Thailand already possesses the nearest army, border intelligence, ceasefire practice, transport knowledge, and political interest. Japan gives Bangkok diplomatic cover to march in and impose operational control.</p>"},
      {id:"nodes",title:"Securing the strategic skeleton",html:"<p>Thai forces prioritize Rangoon, principal airfields and ports, the Irrawaddy transport system, trunk railways, arsenals, heavy-weapons depots, and the principal eastern crossings rather than occupying every village.</p>"},
      {id:"armies",title:"Restriction of the armies",html:"<p>Union and State Guard heavy weapons are registered, limited, centralized, or stored under Thai supervision. Local infantry and police survive, but large inter-state movements require external approval.</p>"},
      {id:"response",title:"Burmese responses",html:"<p>Foreign occupation is humiliating, yet nationality governments fear that united resistance would restore Bamar command or empower a rival frontier state. Anti-Thai officers cannot easily construct an all-Burmese front.</p>"},
      {id:"outcome",title:"Outcome",html:"<p>The crisis ends with formal Burmese sovereignty beneath the <a href='burmese-protection-mandate.html'>Burmese Protection Mandate</a>. Thailand gains the western burden; Japan retains the external guarantee.</p>"}
    ],related:[...burmeseMandateRelated,{href:"thai-intervention-burma.html",kicker:"Decisive operation",label:"Thai intervention"},{href:"war-of-burmese-states.html",kicker:"Earlier failure",label:"War of the Burmese States"}],
    facts:[["Approximate period","1994–1997"],["Theater","Union of Burma"],["Immediate trigger","Open"],["Strategic decision","Japan rejects another internal settlement"],["Intervening power","Thailand"],["Secured assets","Capital, transport, airfields, ports, and arsenals"],["Army settlement","Heavy weapons restricted"],["Outcome","Burmese Protection Mandate"]]
  }),

  "thai-intervention-burma":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Thai Intervention in Burma",eyebrow:"Final Burmese Crisis · Thai military intervention",infoboxTitle:"Thai intervention in Burma",flag:{src:"assets/flags/thailand.svg",alt:"Flag of Thailand",caption:"Thailand intervenes with Japanese diplomatic authorization but does not annex Burma."},
    lead:"The Thai intervention in Burma was the Japanese-authorized military and administrative operation that ended the Final Burmese Crisis and established Thai supervision of the Burmese security order. Thai forces secured the capital, transport systems, airfields, ports, arsenals, and crossings while leaving most rural government in Burmese hands.",
    canon:"Japanese authorization, Thai entry, strategic-node priority, heavy-armament control, survival of local governments, no annexation, and transition to the mandate are established. Operational name, opening date, routes, participating units, commanders, resistance, casualties, and exact duration remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"authorization",title:"Japanese authorization",html:"<p>Tokyo gives Bangkok diplomatic backing after concluding that it will no longer mediate another Burmese constitutional experiment. The authorization deters outside intervention and defines annexation as outside the mission.</p>"},
      {id:"objectives",title:"Objectives",html:"<p>Thailand must stop operational-scale fighting, control heavy weapons, secure external alignment, reopen strategic transport, and impose an arbitration system above the rival national governments.</p>"},
      {id:"entry",title:"Entry and concentration",html:"<p>Thai forces enter chiefly from the eastern frontier and through already familiar liaison routes. They concentrate on command and transport nodes rather than treating Burma as empty territory to be conquered district by district.</p>"},
      {id:"infrastructure",title:"Strategic infrastructure",html:"<p>Rangoon, principal ports, airfields, trunk rail and river systems, heavy arsenals, and major crossings receive Thai garrisons, monitors, or supervisory staffs.</p>"},
      {id:"local",title:"Local administration retained",html:"<p>Burmese governments continue most policing, village administration, schools, temples, local courts, taxation, and routine services. Thai control is concentrated at the common strategic level.</p>"},
      {id:"disarmament",title:"Restriction rather than universal disarmament",html:"<p>State forces retain infantry and local-security functions. Heavy artillery, armor, aviation, strategic communications, and unapproved inter-state troop movements pass under Thai supervision.</p>"},
      {id:"resistance",title:"Fragmented resistance",html:"<p>Bamar nationalists and displaced officers form important anti-Thai currents, but frontier peoples fear that a unified liberation command would become a vehicle for restored Bamar domination.</p>"},
      {id:"settlement",title:"From intervention to mandate",html:"<p>Military control becomes institutional through the High Commission, Council of Burmese States, Security Command, and Japanese guarantee. The intervention therefore ends without annexation or full withdrawal.</p>"}
    ],related:[...burmeseMandateRelated,{href:"final-burmese-crisis.html",kicker:"Parent crisis",label:"Final Burmese Crisis"},{href:"thai-security-command-burma.html",kicker:"Permanent successor",label:"Thai Security Command"}],
    facts:[["Date","Approximately 1994–1997; exact date open"],["Intervening state","Thailand"],["Diplomatic authorization","Japan"],["Primary targets","Strategic infrastructure and heavy weapons"],["Local government","Retained"],["Thai settlement program","None"],["Annexation","None"],["Outcome","Protection mandate"]]
  }),

  "thai-high-commission-burma":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Thai High Commission in Burma",eyebrow:"Rangoon · Executive supervision · Late 1990s onward",infoboxTitle:"Thai High Commission in Burma",
    lead:"The Thai High Commission in Burma is the working title for the executive institution supervising the Burmese Protection Mandate from Rangoon. Headed by a senior Thai royal, soldier, or administrator, it controls common strategic questions while deliberately avoiding ordinary rule over Burmese schools, temples, villages, and local courts.",
    canon:"The Rangoon-based executive body, senior Thai head, supervisory fields, limited ordinary local role, relationship with Burmese governments, and accountability to Bangkok under Japanese guarantee are established. Formal title, headquarters building, first commissioner, appointment term, staff organization, and legal immunities remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"office",title:"Office and seat",html:"<p>The High Commission sits in Rangoon close to the nominal Union government and strategic ministries. Its position above the Burmese council is more important than the exact official residence, which remains open.</p>"},
      {id:"commissioner",title:"The High Commissioner",html:"<p>The commissioner is expected to possess royal, military, or senior administrative standing sufficient to command Thai agencies and receive Burmese state leaders without appearing as a mere ambassador.</p>"},
      {id:"security",title:"Common security",html:"<p>The office confirms ceasefires, troop movements, security appointments, emergency measures, and the relationship between Burmese forces and the Thai Security Command.</p>"},
      {id:"diplomacy",title:"External diplomacy",html:"<p>Burma retains international personality, but foreign alignment, security agreements, bases, and major external commitments require Thai supervision and ultimate consistency with the Japanese guarantee.</p>"},
      {id:"budget",title:"Budget settlement",html:"<p>The commission arbitrates the division of common revenue, security expense, transport maintenance, subsidies, and transfers among Rangoon and the nationality governments.</p>"},
      {id:"transport",title:"Corridors and infrastructure",html:"<p>Trunk railway, river, port, airfield, border-crossing, and heavy-infrastructure decisions fall within the commission's confirmation power because each can alter the military balance.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Limits of ordinary government",html:"<p>The commissioner does not normally appoint village heads, direct worship, design local curricula, or replace every Burmese court with a Thai tribunal. Preserving local government reduces the staff burden and protects the mandate's legitimacy.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Political role",html:"<p>The office distributes concessions and blame: cooperative governments receive roads, trade access, or administrative latitude; renewed mobilization brings funding restrictions, transport controls, or security supervision.</p>"}
    ],related:[...burmeseMandateRelated,{href:"council-burmese-states.html",kicker:"Burmese counterpart",label:"Council of Burmese States"},{href:"thai-administration-burma.html",kicker:"Governing method",label:"Thai administration of Burma"}],
    facts:[["Seat","Rangoon"],["Head","Senior Thai royal, soldier, or administrator"],["Formal title","Working"],["Supervises","Security, diplomacy, budget, corridors, and disputes"],["Confirms","Common security appointments"],["Ordinary local government","Usually Burmese"],["Protecting government","Thailand"],["Ultimate constraint","Japanese guarantee"]]
  }),

  "council-burmese-states":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Council of Burmese States",eyebrow:"Burmese Protection Mandate · Common forum",infoboxTitle:"Council of Burmese States",portrait:{src:"assets/diagrams/burmese-federal-architecture.svg",alt:"Diagram of the Burmese common and constituent institutions",caption:"The council preserves nationality governments within a Thai-confirmed common order."},
    lead:"The Council of Burmese States is the working title for the common Burmese forum under the Protection Mandate. It represents the Bamar core, Shan, Kachin, Chin, Karen, Karenni, Mon, Arakanese, and other recognized governments without granting any one of them control of the common apex.",
    canon:"The multi-government council, represented nationalities, common-question remit, Thai confirmation for strategic matters, survival of state administrations, and lack of independent control over heavy security are established. Formal name, chamber composition, voting rule, delegate numbers, presidency, committees, and meeting site remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"membership",title:"Membership",html:"<p>The council includes the Bamar Union core and the recognized Shan, Kachin, Chin, Karen, Karenni, Mon, and Arakanese governments, with provision for other recognized frontier or autonomous authorities.</p>"},
      {id:"purpose",title:"Purpose",html:"<p>It supplies a Burmese venue for budgets, transit, public works, refugee questions, economic coordination, and disputes that cannot be handled by one state alone.</p>"},
      {id:"equality",title:"Political equality and practical inequality",html:"<p>Formal representation prevents Rangoon from claiming the council as a disguised central ministry. Population, wealth, geography, military position, and Thai patronage nevertheless make the members unequal in practice.</p>"},
      {id:"confirmation",title:"Thai confirmation",html:"<p>Decisions affecting security, foreign relations, heavy infrastructure, strategic transport, or inter-state boundaries require confirmation by the High Commission.</p>"},
      {id:"states",title:"Powers retained by the states",html:"<p>Member governments preserve land, language, religion, schools, ordinary policing, local courts, municipal administration, and State Guard or equivalent local-security institutions.</p>"},
      {id:"withheld",title:"Powers withheld",html:"<p>The council cannot freely authorize foreign bases, distribute heavy weapons, change alignment, direct military aviation, or order operational-scale movement among state territories.</p>"},
      {id:"disputes",title:"A forum for managed accusation",html:"<p>Delegates continue blaming one another for the intervention. The council channels that conflict into hearings and bargaining while the Thai chair or commissioner prevents accusation from becoming mobilization.</p>"},
      {id:"meaning",title:"Meaning",html:"<p>The council preserves Burmese political particularism after self-government has been curtailed. Critics call it a parliament beneath a foreign veto; supporters call it the only forum that does not make one nationality master of the others.</p>"}
    ],related:[...burmeseMandateRelated,{href:"thai-high-commission-burma.html",kicker:"Confirming authority",label:"Thai High Commission"},{href:"inter-nationality-politics-burmese-mandate.html",kicker:"Political dynamic",label:"Inter-nationality politics"}],
    facts:[["Status","Common Burmese forum"],["Formal title","Working"],["Principal members","Bamar, Shan, Kachin, Chin, Karen, Karenni, Mon, and Arakanese governments"],["Thai role","Confirmation of strategic decisions"],["Local institutions","Retained"],["Independent heavy-weapons authority","None"],["Independent foreign alignment","None"],["Voting system","Open"]]
  }),

  "thai-security-command-burma":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Thai Security Command in Burma",eyebrow:"Burmese Protection Mandate · Common security",infoboxTitle:"Thai Security Command in Burma",flag:{src:"assets/flags/thailand.svg",alt:"Flag of Thailand",caption:"Thai command authority covers Burma's strategic military system rather than its entire local administration."},
    lead:"The Thai Security Command in Burma is the working title for the mandate institution controlling heavy weapons, aviation, strategic communications, principal arsenals, trunk transport, ceasefire observation, and inter-state troop movement. It leaves ordinary infantry, police, border guards, and local-security work to Burmese forces under registration and supervision.",
    canon:"The command's control fields, registration and storage of Burmese heavy weapons, survival of local forces, ceasefire and movement functions, Thai leadership, and Japanese strategic oversight are established. Formal title, command hierarchy, bases, unit composition, force ceilings, weapons schedules, and rules of engagement remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"mission",title:"Mission",html:"<p>The command prevents any Burmese army from converting a local dispute into an operational campaign before the High Commission can arbitrate.</p>"},
      {id:"heavy",title:"Heavy-weapons control",html:"<p>Armor, major artillery, military aviation, strategic air defense, and large ammunition reserves are limited, registered, stored, or operated beneath Thai supervision.</p>"},
      {id:"communications",title:"Strategic communications",html:"<p>Common command networks, long-range radio, major switching nodes, and selected surveillance systems remain outside unilateral Burmese control.</p>"},
      {id:"transport",title:"Transport security",html:"<p>Trunk railway, Irrawaddy river traffic, principal airfields, ports, and eastern crossings receive security commands because controlling movement is often more decisive than controlling territory.</p>"},
      {id:"ceasefire",title:"Ceasefire observation",html:"<p>Thai monitors inspect lines, investigate mobilization, and report violations. Their presence gives rival governments a common external witness while also making Thailand judge of its own coercive settlement.</p>"},
      {id:"local",title:"Burmese forces retained",html:"<p>Union and state formations retain infantry, scouts, police, border guards, gendarmerie, and local transport. They may not move at operational scale across another member's territory without approval.</p>"},
      {id:"emergency",title:"Emergency intervention",html:"<p>Thai units can reinforce infrastructure, separate forces, seize unregistered arms, or assume temporary local command during a major breach. Exact legal thresholds remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Limits and legitimacy",html:"<p>The command is effective because it controls escalation, not because it disarms every village. It is resented as foreign military rule and defended as the mechanism preventing another Burmese war.</p>"}
    ],related:[...burmeseMandateRelated,{href:"armed-federalism-burma.html",kicker:"Pre-mandate order",label:"Armed Federalism"},{href:"thai-intervention-burma.html",kicker:"Military origin",label:"Thai intervention"}],
    facts:[["Status","Mandate security authority"],["Formal title","Working"],["Commanding state","Thailand"],["Controls","Heavy weapons and military aviation"],["Secures","Strategic communications and trunk transport"],["Observes","Ceasefires and troop movements"],["Burmese forces retain","Infantry, police, border, and local security"],["Ultimate strategic guarantor","Japan"]]
  }),

  "japanese-guarantee-burma":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Japanese Guarantee of the Burmese Mandate",eyebrow:"GEACPS · External guarantee · Late 1990s onward",infoboxTitle:"Japanese guarantee of Burma",
    lead:"The Japanese guarantee of the Burmese Mandate is the external strategic layer that protects Burma and validates Thai supervision while preventing Bangkok from annexing the country or converting delegated responsibility into an independent mainland empire. Japan delegates the burden of rule without surrendering final primacy.",
    canon:"Japan's ultimate-guarantor role, deterrence of outside intervention, prohibition of Thai annexation, continued Burmese membership in the Japanese system, support and supply leverage, and delegated-not-abandoned doctrine are established. Legal instrument, explicit enforcement clauses, stationed forces, inspection rights, and crisis procedures remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"delegation",title:"Delegation without withdrawal",html:"<p>Tokyo authorizes Thailand because local knowledge and proximity make Bangkok the cheaper daily supervisor. Japan remains responsible for the international settlement.</p>"},
      {id:"external",title:"External defense",html:"<p>The guarantee warns India, China, Britain, Germany, and other powers that the Burmese crisis is not an opening for intervention, bases, or territorial revision.</p>"},
      {id:"annexation",title:"Barrier to Thai annexation",html:"<p>Thailand may supervise security and alignment but may not absorb Burmese land, convert citizens into Thai subjects, or place Burma outside the Japanese-led system.</p>"},
      {id:"recognition",title:"Recognition and legitimacy",html:"<p>Japan recognizes Burma's continuing international personality and the protected nationality governments. That recognition distinguishes the mandate from outright conquest even where practical sovereignty is narrow.</p>"},
      {id:"supply",title:"Supply and finance",html:"<p>Japanese equipment, banks, corporate networks, and development finance help sustain Thai administration. This dependence gives Tokyo leverage over the supervising power.</p>"},
      {id:"restraint",title:"Restraint of the Burmese forces",html:"<p>Japanese backing makes Thai weapons registration and movement controls credible against governments that might otherwise wait for Bangkok's attention to lapse.</p>"},
      {id:"sphere",title:"Meaning inside the Sphere",html:"<p>The guarantee proves that association can end in delegated foreign supervision when repeated internal collapse threatens strategic order. It preserves the legal categories of particularism while weakening their moral promise.</p>"},
      {id:"primacy",title:"Final primacy",html:"<p>Thailand carries the officers, translators, garrisons, and political cost. Japan retains the power to define the mandate's outer limits and the conditions under which it remains legitimate.</p>"}
    ],related:[...burmeseMandateRelated,{href:"burma-preservationist-break.html",kicker:"Doctrinal effect",label:"The preservationist break"},{href:"geacps.html",kicker:"Imperial system",label:"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere"}],
    facts:[["Guarantor","Empire of Japan"],["Daily protecting power","Thailand"],["Protected international personality","Union of Burma"],["External intervention","Deterred"],["Thai annexation","Prohibited"],["Burma leaves GEACPS","Prohibited"],["Material leverage","Supply, finance, and recognition"],["Doctrine","Delegated burden; retained primacy"]]
  }),

  "thai-administration-burma":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Thai Administration of Burma",eyebrow:"Burmese Protection Mandate · Administrative practice",infoboxTitle:"Thai administration in Burma",
    lead:"Thai administration of Burma is the limited, supervisory method by which Bangkok preserves local governments, rewards compliance, controls strategic functions, and prevents a common anti-Thai coalition. It avoids replacing every Burmese institution because doing so would exceed Thai capacity and unite the nationalities against foreign rule.",
    canon:"Preservation of local government, distribution of concessions and sanctions, strategic rather than universal administration, prevention of a unified resistance, reliance on Thai officers and specialists, and continuing local resentment are established. Ministry boundaries, provincial offices, staffing totals, administrative language rules, and detailed civil-service law remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"local",title:"Local governments preserved",html:"<p>Thai officials leave most schools, temples, villages, municipal work, ordinary courts, land administration, and local policing to Bamar and nationality governments.</p>"},
      {id:"strategic",title:"Strategic supervision",html:"<p>The High Commission and Security Command concentrate on security, diplomacy, heavy infrastructure, trunk transport, arsenals, budgets, and inter-state disputes.</p>"},
      {id:"rewards",title:"Concessions and rewards",html:"<p>Cooperative governments may receive roads, trade access, investment, subsidies, administrative latitude, or a larger role in local appointments.</p>"},
      {id:"sanctions",title:"Sanctions for renewed mobilization",html:"<p>A state that reopens fighting can lose funds, transport privileges, weapons access, command authority, or control of specified security forces.</p>"},
      {id:"staff",title:"The Thai administrative corps",html:"<p>Officers, police, translators, engineers, railway managers, judges, legal advisers, health and refugee personnel, treasury officials, and royal or military patrons rotate through the mandate.</p>"},
      {id:"language",title:"Language and mediation",html:"<p>Thai is the language of the supervising administration but does not replace Burmese or the protected nationality languages as ordinary public speech. Interpretation is a permanent state function.</p>"},
      {id:"resistance",title:"Preventing a common front",html:"<p>The administration emphasizes that Thai supervision restrains every rival. Nationality governments fear that a single liberation movement would become a vehicle for the strongest Burmese claimant.</p>"},
      {id:"judgment",title:"Administrative judgment",html:"<p>The method succeeds at preventing large wars more clearly than at producing loyalty. It is cheaper than direct annexation but permanently absorbs Thai attention.</p>"}
    ],related:[...burmeseMandateRelated,{href:"thai-high-commission-burma.html",kicker:"Executive institution",label:"Thai High Commission"},{href:"thailand-burmese-burden.html",kicker:"Metropolitan cost",label:"Thailand's Burmese burden"}],
    facts:[["Administrative model","Strategic supervision with local government retained"],["Ordinary village government","Burmese"],["Principal Thai offices","High Commission and Security Command"],["Reward instruments","Infrastructure, trade, subsidy, and latitude"],["Sanctions","Funds, transport, arms, and command restrictions"],["Administrative language","Thai within supervision; local languages retained"],["Settlement policy","No mass Thai colonization"],["Political result","Stability without loyalty"]]
  }),

  "inter-nationality-politics-burmese-mandate":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Inter-nationality Politics under the Burmese Mandate",eyebrow:"Burmese Protection Mandate · Political sociology",infoboxTitle:"Inter-nationality politics under the mandate",
    lead:"Inter-nationality politics under the Burmese Mandate is defined by a paradox: Thai rule is foreign and humiliating, yet it prevents any Burmese nationality from converting the common state into its own national government. Each group consequently explains foreign supervision as the result of another group's misconduct, obstructing both renewed civil war and a common liberation movement.",
    canon:"The external-apex logic, mutual attribution of blame, Bamar and frontier narratives, difficulty of an all-Burmese anti-Thai movement, local-government survival, and monarchy-like political function of the Thai Crown are established. Named parties, slogans, opinion totals, insurgent organizations, and regional variations remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"capital",title:"The capital problem",html:"<p>Every previous settlement made the people holding the capital appear to govern the others. Rangoon's Bamar location therefore transformed federal institutions into evidence of Bamar supremacy even where their formal powers were limited.</p>"},
      {id:"external",title:"An external apex",html:"<p>The Thai High Commissioner belongs to none of the Burmese nationalities. Submission to the commission does not require a Shan, Karen, Kachin, or Mon official to admit Bamar mastery.</p>"},
      {id:"monarchy",title:"A monarchy-like function",html:"<p>The Chakri king does not become King of Burma, but the Thai Crown supplies continuity and dignity above the competing domestic estates in a manner analogous to a common monarch.</p>"},
      {id:"bamar",title:"Bamar narratives",html:"<p>Bamar politicians commonly blame frontier separatism, state armies, and repeated refusal of union authority for making foreign intervention possible.</p>"},
      {id:"frontier",title:"Frontier narratives",html:"<p>Shan, Karen, Kachin, Chin, Mon, Karenni, and Arakanese leaders point instead to Bamar centralization, military movement, and refusal of genuine equality.</p>"},
      {id:"smaller",title:"Smaller-state narratives",html:"<p>Smaller peoples blame both the Bamar center and the stronger frontier states, militia commanders, or neighboring governments that broke the previous bargain.</p>"},
      {id:"resistance",title:"Limits of anti-Thai resistance",html:"<p>Anti-Thai insurgents exist, especially among displaced officers and Bamar nationalists. Other groups fear that liberation under a unified command would merely restore the old hierarchy.</p>"},
      {id:"equilibrium",title:"The mandate equilibrium",html:"<p>The usual conclusion is not that Thailand conquered a united Burmese people, but that Thailand would not need to be present if rival Burmese actors had obeyed the last settlement. That attribution is the mandate's strongest political defense.</p>"}
    ],related:[...burmeseMandateRelated,{href:"council-burmese-states.html",kicker:"Institutional forum",label:"Council of Burmese States"},{href:"thai-administration-burma.html",kicker:"Supervisory practice",label:"Thai administration"}],
    facts:[["Central dynamic","Mutual attribution of blame"],["Common apex","Thai and external"],["Bamar argument","Frontier separatism caused intervention"],["Frontier argument","Bamar centralization caused intervention"],["Smaller-state fear","Domination by larger Burmese claimants"],["Unified anti-Thai movement","Difficult to sustain"],["Local governments","Preserved"],["Political result","Foreign supervision without domestic national victor"]]
  }),

  "thailand-burmese-burden":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Thailand's Burmese Burden",eyebrow:"Kingdom of Thailand · Late 1990s onward",infoboxTitle:"Thailand's Burmese burden",flag:{src:"assets/flags/thailand.svg",alt:"Flag of Thailand",caption:"The mandate enlarges Thai power while narrowing the purposes to which that power can be applied."},
    lead:"Thailand's Burmese burden is the political and administrative cost created by the Protection Mandate. Bangkok gains prestige, strategic depth, resources, and privileged contracts, but Burma absorbs officers, police, translators, engineers, judges, health services, treasury staff, patronage, and diplomatic attention on a scale comparable to Thailand's existing imperial system.",
    canon:"The initial nationalist triumphalism, large administrative burden, required professional categories, defensive narrowing of Thai diplomacy, resource gains, unequal distribution of benefits and costs, reduced wider-Sphere assertiveness, and Malayan autonomy consequence are established. Fiscal totals, troop numbers, ministries, political factions, scandals, and yearly costs remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"triumph",title:"The apparent triumph",html:"<p>Thai nationalists initially claim that Bangkok has solved a problem Japan failed to master over four decades and proved its superior knowledge of mainland government.</p>"},
      {id:"scale",title:"The scale of Burma",html:"<p>Burma is not another small protectorate. Its territory, governments, languages, armies, religious institutions, and unresolved frontiers are comparable in burden to much of the Thai imperial system itself.</p>"},
      {id:"people",title:"Personnel absorbed",html:"<p>The mandate consumes army officers, police, intelligence staff, interpreters, engineers, railway administrators, judges, legal advisers, health and refugee services, treasury officials, and court or military patronage.</p>"},
      {id:"career",title:"The mandate career",html:"<p>A Thai official may rotate for most of a career among Shan ceasefire districts, Rangoon headquarters, Karen crossings, Arakan emergency missions, and Bangkok review offices.</p>"},
      {id:"triple",title:"The three consequences of every decision",html:"<p>Each administrative choice must be judged for its local effect, the interpretation placed upon it by rival Burmese nationalities, and Japan's view of its consistency with the guarantee.</p>"},
      {id:"diplomacy",title:"Withdrawal from wider Sphere politics",html:"<p>Thailand shifts from demanding a larger part in directing GEACPS toward defending its freedom to manage Burma, secure Japanese funding and equipment, restrict outside criticism, and control refugee flows.</p>"},
      {id:"malaya",title:"The Malayan consequence",html:"<p>Bangkok cannot administer Burma intensively while closely supervising Malaya. The <a href='malayan-autonomy-settlement.html'>late-1990s autonomy settlement</a> transfers more domestic powers to the protected Malayan kingdom.</p>"},
      {id:"formula",title:"Victory and ball-and-chain",html:"<p>Burma is an unapologetic Thai imperial victory and a permanent restraint. It makes Thailand wealthier and territorially more important while limiting what Bangkok can do with that strength.</p>"}
    ],related:[...burmeseMandateRelated,{href:"thai-burmese-concession-economy.html",kicker:"Material balance",label:"Thai–Burmese concession economy"},{href:"malayan-autonomy-settlement.html",kicker:"Indirect consequence",label:"Malayan autonomy settlement"}],
    facts:[["Period","Late 1990s onward"],["Initial political mood","National triumph"],["Principal cost","Administrative attention"],["Personnel demand","Military, police, technical, legal, health, and fiscal"],["Economic effect","Resource and contract gains"],["Diplomatic effect","Narrower and more defensive"],["Regional consequence","Greater Malayan autonomy"],["Common description","Victory and burden"]]
  }),

  "thai-burmese-concession-economy":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Thai–Burmese Concession Economy",eyebrow:"Burmese Protection Mandate · Resources and development",infoboxTitle:"Thai–Burmese concession economy",
    lead:"The Thai–Burmese concession economy is the system of resource access, transport, infrastructure, and protected contracts that makes the Burmese Mandate materially valuable to Thailand. Thai firms, military-linked enterprises, court interests, Japanese banks, and zaibatsu profit from timber, energy, gems, rice, hydropower, ports, and construction while the Thai state carries the cost of garrisons and administration.",
    canon:"The principal resources, privileged Thai access, partnership with Japanese capital, gains to connected firms and elites, public security and administrative costs, infrastructure role, and unequal distribution of benefit are established. Concession names, corporate shares, production totals, taxation, corruption cases, and precise budget balance remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"resources",title:"Resource base",html:"<p>The mandate opens privileged access to timber, petroleum and gas, gems and minerals, rice, hydropower, ports, transit, construction, and communications contracts.</p>"},
      {id:"firms",title:"Thai firms and military networks",html:"<p>Thai companies and military-linked development organizations expand westward, often using security relationships and transport concessions as the basis for commercial access.</p>"},
      {id:"japan",title:"Japanese capital",html:"<p>Japanese banks and zaibatsu supply finance, equipment, insurance, processing links, and export markets. Their participation also keeps Thailand dependent upon Tokyo's approval.</p>"},
      {id:"corridors",title:"Corridor development",html:"<p>Roads, trunk rail, river ports, border crossings, pipelines, power projects, and telecommunications serve both economic extraction and the mandate's security system.</p>"},
      {id:"winners",title:"Concentrated winners",html:"<p>Connected business groups, court patrons, officers, concession holders, and cooperating Burmese authorities receive the most visible gains.</p>"},
      {id:"costs",title:"Socialized costs",html:"<p>The Thai treasury funds garrisons, administrators, subsidies, refugee response, damaged infrastructure, and repeated security operations whose benefits are not distributed equally.</p>"},
      {id:"burmese",title:"Burmese participation",html:"<p>Nationality governments bargain for royalties, roads, employment, and local contracts. The High Commission can use access as a reward for compliance, linking development to political discipline.</p>"},
      {id:"balance",title:"The mandate balance",html:"<p>Burma pays Thailand in resources and costs Thailand in attention. The formula explains why Bangkok keeps the mandate despite persistent complaint and why the arrangement does not translate automatically into wider Thai freedom of action.</p>"}
    ],related:[...burmeseMandateRelated,{href:"thailand-burmese-burden.html",kicker:"Political cost",label:"Thailand's Burmese burden"},{href:"thai-administration-burma.html",kicker:"Administrative system",label:"Thai administration"}],
    facts:[["Principal beneficiary","Thailand"],["External capital partner","Japan"],["Resources","Timber, energy, gems, rice, and hydropower"],["Strategic assets","Ports, transit, construction, and communications"],["Private winners","Connected firms and patrons"],["Public costs","Garrisons, administration, subsidy, and security"],["Burmese bargaining","Royalties, jobs, roads, and contracts"],["Political formula","Resources for Thailand; attention consumed by Burma"]]
  }),

  "malayan-autonomy-settlement":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Malayan Autonomy Settlement",eyebrow:"Federated Kingdom of Malaya · Late 1990s",infoboxTitle:"Malayan Autonomy Settlement",
    lead:"The Malayan Autonomy Settlement is the late-1990s transfer of substantial domestic authority from Thailand to the protected Federated Kingdom of Malaya. It results directly from Bangkok's inability to supervise Burma intensively while retaining the same control over Malaya, turning Thai domination of one protectorate into greater self-government for the other.",
    canon:"The late-1990s timing, Burmese administrative cause, expanded Malayan powers over taxation, policing, courts, education, commercial law, local rail administration, and appointments, continued Thai control of defense and foreign relations, and protected-not-fully-independent status are established. Formal title, date, monarch, cabinet, customs division, constitutional text, and enforcement clauses remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"background",title:"The two-protectorate problem",html:"<p>Before the Burmese Mandate, Thailand supervises Malaya closely while playing an assertive role across mainland GEACPS. Burma consumes the officials, military attention, and patronage required to sustain both systems.</p>"},
      {id:"tax",title:"Taxation and appointments",html:"<p>Malayan authorities receive greater control over internal taxation, domestic appointments, and the ordinary distribution of administrative revenue.</p>"},
      {id:"law",title:"Police, courts, and commercial law",html:"<p>Policing, courts, education, commercial law, and local railway administration pass more fully to Malayan institutions.</p>"},
      {id:"customs",title:"Customs",html:"<p>Portions of customs administration may enter the settlement, but the exact boundary between domestic revenue and Thai strategic control remains open.</p>"},
      {id:"thai",title:"Thai reserved powers",html:"<p>Thailand retains external defense, foreign relations, strategic bases, military transit, restrictions on hostile alliances, and intervention rights during constitutional collapse.</p>"},
      {id:"status",title:"A protected kingdom",html:"<p>The settlement does not create full independence. It changes Malaya from a closely supervised client into a substantially self-governing protected kingdom.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Thai debate",html:"<p>Some nationalists oppose retreat; others welcome the chance to concentrate upon the far more demanding Burmese mandate and treat Malaya as the manageable protectorate.</p>"},
      {id:"meaning",title:"The Burmese irony",html:"<p>Burma enlarges Thai empire in the west while forcing decentralization in the south. Administrative capacity, rather than constitutional principle alone, produces Malayan self-government.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"malaya.html",kicker:"Protected state",label:"Federated Kingdom of Malaya"},{href:"thailand.html",kicker:"Protecting power",label:"Kingdom of Thailand"},{href:"thailand-burmese-burden.html",kicker:"Immediate cause",label:"Thailand's Burmese burden"},{href:"burmese-protection-mandate.html",kicker:"Regional context",label:"Burmese Protection Mandate"}],
    facts:[["Period","Late 1990s"],["Protected state","Federated Kingdom of Malaya"],["Protecting power","Thailand"],["Immediate cause","Administrative burden of Burma"],["Domestic gains","Taxation, policing, courts, education, and commercial law"],["Thai reserved powers","Defense, foreign affairs, bases, and transit"],["Customs division","Open"],["Result","Substantially self-governing protected kingdom"]]
  }),

  "azad-hind-lost-victory":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Azad Hind's Lost Victory",eyebrow:"Indian Wars · 1952–1956 · Political memory",infoboxTitle:"Azad Hind's Lost Victory",
    lead:"Azad Hind's Lost Victory is the national interpretation of the 1954–1956 reversal in which the Burmese Federal Crisis broke the overland supply system supporting Azad Hind's high-water mark against Congress India. Calcutta does not accuse Japan of deliberate betrayal; it blames Burmese disorder for consuming the ammunition, transport, aircraft, engineers, communications, and attention that might have enabled an all-Indian victory.",
    canon:"Azad Hind's 1952–1956 high-water mark, credible prospect of defeating Congress India in the east and north, breakdown of the Burma–Assam ammunition corridor, ill-advised final attacks, Congress counteroffensive, retreat toward Bengal and Calcutta, blame of Burma rather than Japan, and long-term refusal of GEACPS membership are established. Battle names, exact front, commanders, force strengths, losses, and wording of the doctrine remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"highwater",title:"The 1952–1956 high-water mark",html:"<p>Japanese matériel, inherited INA institutions, armored capability, and a functioning overland supply route give Azad Hind a real chance to defeat the Congress-led Indian Union across the east and north.</p>"},
      {id:"crisis",title:"The Burmese Federal Crisis",html:"<p>Fighting in Burma interrupts roads, railways, depots, convoys, aircraft support, engineering, communications, and political attention. Equipment is not confiscated from Azad Hind; the system delivering consumables fails.</p>"},
      {id:"ammunition",title:"The ammunition crisis",html:"<p>Accumulated stocks permit operations through much of 1955. By 1956 ammunition has become the decisive constraint even as Japanese advisers and Azad Hind commanders believe victory remains close.</p>"},
      {id:"offensive",title:"The final offensive",html:"<p>Azad Hind launches attacks intended to break the remaining Congress resistance before stocks run out. Overextension and ammunition scarcity turn the gamble into failure.</p>"},
      {id:"counter",title:"Congress counteroffensive",html:"<p>Congress forces drive Azad Hind back toward earlier defensive positions and eastern depots near Bengal and Calcutta. Restored Japanese supply later prevents destruction but cannot restore the lost monopoly of advanced matériel.</p>"},
      {id:"blame",title:"Burma, not Japan",html:"<p>The official and popular grievance treats Japan as an ally frustrated by the same crisis. Burma becomes the political space whose repeated disorder cost India its moment of unity.</p>"},
      {id:"sphere",title:"Refusal of Sphere membership",html:"<p>Azad Hind remains a bilateral Japanese associate because it refuses to recognize Burma as a partner, assume collective obligations toward it, or enter Thai-influenced mainland institutions.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy after the mandate",html:"<p>Thai pacification brings practical relief but not reconciliation. To Azad Hind, the mandate proves that decades of Japanese effort in Burma ended in foreign supervision after already costing India its victory.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"azad-hind.html",kicker:"Remembering state",label:"Azad Hind"},{href:"burma-assam-ammunition-corridor.html",kicker:"Material cause",label:"Burma–Assam ammunition corridor"},{href:"burmese-federal-crisis.html",kicker:"Decisive interruption",label:"Burmese Federal Crisis"},{href:"congress-india.html",kicker:"Principal opponent",label:"Congress India"}],
    facts:[["High-water period","1952–1956"],["Principal opponent","Congress India"],["Supply route","Burma–Assam corridor"],["Disrupting event","Burmese Federal Crisis"],["Decisive shortage","Ammunition"],["Military result","Retreat toward Bengal and Calcutta"],["State blamed","Burma"],["Long-term diplomatic result","Azad Hind remains outside GEACPS membership"]]
  }),

  "burma-assam-ammunition-corridor":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Burma–Assam Ammunition Corridor",eyebrow:"Japanese–Azad Hind logistics · Early and mid-1950s",infoboxTitle:"Burma–Assam ammunition corridor",
    lead:"The Burma–Assam ammunition corridor was the overland road, rail, depot, convoy, air-support, and communications system through which Japan sustained Azad Hind during its 1952–1956 high-water mark. Its breakdown during the Burmese Federal Crisis, rather than the confiscation of Azad Hind's existing equipment, caused the ammunition shortage behind the Lost Victory.",
    canon:"The overland route, use of Burma and Assam approaches, preference over exposed naval supply, transport and depot structure, interruption by the Federal Crisis, delayed stock exhaustion, effect on Azad Hind operations, and later partial restoration are established. Exact roads, railheads, port of entry, depot names, tonnage, convoy schedules, and attacking forces remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"purpose",title:"Purpose",html:"<p>Japan needs a sustained route for ammunition, fuel, spare parts, radios, engineering stores, advisers, and selected armored or aviation support to Calcutta's expanding army.</p>"},
      {id:"route",title:"Overland architecture",html:"<p>Sea entry, Burmese trunk transport, depots, road and rail transfer, Assam approaches, local convoy systems, airfields, and communications form one logistical chain rather than a single historic road.</p>"},
      {id:"sea",title:"Why not rely on sea supply",html:"<p>Overland movement reduces exposure to submarines, raiders, and interception against the long maritime approach to Bengal while connecting Japanese-controlled mainland infrastructure.</p>"},
      {id:"consumables",title:"Consumables over equipment",html:"<p>Azad Hind retains weapons already delivered. The corridor's decisive output is the repeated movement of ammunition, fuel, parts, maintenance stores, and specialist support required to keep those weapons useful.</p>"},
      {id:"crisis",title:"Breakdown in 1954–1956",html:"<p>Federal fighting blocks routes, threatens depots, diverts transport and engineers, disrupts communications, and redirects Japanese aircraft and political attention.</p>"},
      {id:"delay",title:"The delayed effect",html:"<p>Stocks accumulated before the crisis sustain the advance through much of 1955. The true operational shock arrives as ammunition runs down in 1956.</p>"},
      {id:"reversal",title:"Azad Hind reversal",html:"<p>Attempts to win before exhaustion fail, and Congress counterattacks push the eastern army back toward depots nearer Calcutta and Bengal.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Restoration and memory",html:"<p>Supply eventually resumes, but other Indian states have acquired and developed Russian-derived weapons and ammunition systems. The corridor never again gives Azad Hind the same temporary monopoly of capability.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"azad-hind-lost-victory.html",kicker:"Strategic consequence",label:"Azad Hind's Lost Victory"},{href:"burmese-federal-crisis.html",kicker:"Disrupting conflict",label:"Burmese Federal Crisis"},{href:"azad-hind.html",kicker:"Principal recipient",label:"Azad Hind"},{href:"japan.html",kicker:"Principal supplier",label:"Empire of Japan"}],
    facts:[["Period","Early to mid-1950s"],["Supplier","Japan"],["Recipient","Azad Hind"],["Geography","Burma and Assam approaches"],["Main cargo","Ammunition and other consumables"],["Disruption","Burmese Federal Crisis, 1954–1956"],["Operational effect","Stock exhaustion in 1956"],["Later status","Restored without recovering former strategic advantage"]]
  }),

  "burma-preservationist-break":burmeseMandateArticle({
    title:"Burma and the Preservationist Break",eyebrow:"Japanese political doctrine · 1985–2000",infoboxTitle:"The Burmese preservationist break",
    lead:"The Burmese preservationist break is the name given to the doctrinal shift revealed when Japan authorized Thai rule over Burma in the mid-1990s. Cultural and national institutions remained protected, but Tokyo abandoned the preservationist assumption that legitimate order must emerge from the protected culture itself. Political function, security, and hierarchy took priority over another internal settlement.",
    canon:"Preservationism's peak around 1985, its connection to Burmese particularist settlements, post-1985 decline, Thai intervention as the clearest break, continued protection of Burmese national institutions, southern-archipelago alarm, and the post-2000 hierarchy-first consensus are established. Name as a formal contemporary doctrine, responsible cabinets, parliamentary debates, polling, and exact ideological factions remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"peak",title:"Preservationism at its peak",html:"<p>Around 1985 Japanese preservationists argue that national homelands, languages, local institutions, and political particularism can coexist with Japanese strategic leadership.</p>"},
      {id:"burma",title:"Burma as the hardest test",html:"<p>Federal autonomy and the Sovereign States Experiment embody particularism at its most ambitious. Repeated wars make Burma evidence that ever-finer political recognition does not guarantee peace.</p>"},
      {id:"decline",title:"Decline after 1985",html:"<p>Limited language revival, unchanged corporate ownership, unstable particularist states, and generational change weaken the movement's authority without abolishing its museums, archives, schools, and cultural programs.</p>"},
      {id:"decision",title:"The Thai decision",html:"<p>A preservationist government at the movement's height would have demanded another localized Burmese settlement. The mid-1990s government instead authorizes an external Thai authority to impose order.</p>"},
      {id:"qualification",title:"The qualification of particularism",html:"<p>Japanese officials argue that national communities retain governments, languages, and institutions, but particularism is protected only while the resulting order remains minimally functional.</p>"},
      {id:"division",title:"Division of governing responsibility",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-b5k8r writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T12:21:59-06:00 --><p>Burmese institutions retained the flag, citizenship, nationality governments, schools, temples, local courts, police, and ordinary administration. The Council of Burmese States supplied a common forum. The Thai High Commission supervised security, diplomacy, common-budget settlement, strategic transport, inter-state disputes, and appointments to common security bodies. The Thai Security Command controlled heavy weapons, military aviation, strategic communications, principal arsenals, trunk routes, and inter-state troop movements. Japan guaranteed the external settlement and prevented either Thai annexation or withdrawal from the Japanese system.</p><p>This division answered the post-preservationist question of institutional capacity. Local administrations could maintain ordinary law and cultural life; the foreign apex could keep heavy forces apart, preserve corridors, allocate common burdens, and impose final arbitration. Success meant fewer operational-scale wars and a system capable of moving relief, revenue, and transport across disputed boundaries. It did not mean Burmese reconciliation or equal sovereignty.</p><p>The settlement treated culture as something that could survive beneath a security order created outside the protected societies. That was the break with preservationist statecraft. It also supplied its strongest moral objection: Burmese institutions could remain active while every decisive question of force, alignment, transport, and arbitration belonged to Thailand under Japanese guarantee. Administrative survival did not by itself legitimate the hierarchy that made it possible.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"critics",title:"Criticism",html:"<p>Critics answer that a foreign High Commission, security command, and strategic veto convert preserved culture into decoration beneath ordinary imperial rule.</p>"},
      {id:"sphere",title:"Shock inside GEACPS",html:"<p>Southern archipelago states see the mandate as proof that enough disorder can exhaust Japanese patience and end meaningful self-government. Manchukuoan and Chinese officials use it to defend administrative unity; the Philippines is more uneasy.</p>"},
      {id:"consensus",title:"The post-preservationist consensus",html:"<p>By 2000 the dominant formula is that cultures should be protected where practical, while political order, economic function, and strategic security come first. Preservation survives as administration rather than imperial moral theory.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Parent movement",label:"Japanese Preservationism"},{href:"burmese-protection-mandate.html",kicker:"Decisive case",label:"Burmese Protection Mandate"},{href:"sovereign-states-experiment.html",kicker:"Earlier high-water mark",label:"Sovereign States Experiment"},{href:"geacps.html",kicker:"Imperial context",label:"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere"}],
    facts:[["Preservationist peak","Around 1985"],["Earlier Burmese expression","Sovereign States Experiment"],["Decisive break","Thai intervention in Burma"],["Approximate break period","Mid-1990s"],["Institutions preserved","Nationality governments, languages, and local administration"],["Strategic apex","Thai under Japanese guarantee"],["Reaction","Alarm across associated states"],["By 2000","Preservation remains administrative, not governing doctrine"]]
  })
});

