addIshikawaBurmeseGuaranteeSection("japanese-guarantee-burma",{id:"enforcement",title:"Enforcement and review",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-b9q5n writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T14:22:30-06:00 --><p>Japan authorized the Thai protectorate in the mid-1990s while Burma remained formally sovereign and Thailand controlled common security, heavy weapons, foreign alignment, strategic transport, and final arbitration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Tokyo's guarantee applied at the outer boundary of that arrangement. It deterred foreign intervention, barred Thai annexation, preserved Burmese international personality, and kept both protecting power and protected states inside the Japanese strategic system.</p><p>Daily complaints and compliance questions passed through Burmese governments, the Council of Burmese States, the Thai High Commission, and the Thai Security Command. A matter reached the Japanese level when it concerned annexation, external bases, withdrawal from the Sphere, collapse of strategic corridors, failure of the Thai command, or a dispute between Bangkok and another associated government. Tokyo's leverage came from recognition, military supply, bank credit, development finance, corporate participation, diplomatic support, and its ability to redefine Thailand's delegated authority. The exact review body and enforcement clauses remain open.</p><p>Post-preservationist officials defended the guarantee by its capacity to keep heavy forces apart, move relief, protect transport, and prevent another operational-scale Burmese war. Local schools, temples, courts, police, and nationality governments could continue beneath that security ceiling. The arrangement also demonstrated the failure of preservationist promises. Burmese cultures and governments survived, yet decisive control of force and alignment belonged to Thailand, while Japan retained the authority to decide whether that domination remained acceptable.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
